.TH CCOL_CREATE_CTHREAD_POOL_MP 3 "2026-09-27" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_create_cthread_pool_mp \- create a thread pool with custom memory management
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

ctpool ccol_create_cthread_pool_mp(size_t num_threads,
                              size_t queue_capacity,
                              ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs,
                              char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a new thread pool with the given number of worker threads.
The queue_capacity parameter selects the task queue mode:

  0 -> unbounded task queue. ctpool_submit never blocks on capacity,
    and the only failure path that does not come from an argument is
    ccol_not_enough_memory.

  N > 0 -> bounded task queue of capacity N. ctpool_submit blocks when
    the queue is full, while ctpool_try_submit does not block and
    returns ccol_container_full instead.

This function starts all num_threads worker threads before it returns.
If a pthread_create call fails, it cleanly joins the threads that have
already started and then returns CTPOOL_INVALID.

Each worker thread records its pool on one thread-specific key that the
library creates for the whole process on the first creation of a pool;
that key is what lets a task that destroys its own pool fail loudly.
When the process has no key left to give (PTHREAD_KEYS_MAX), this
function returns CTPOOL_INVALID, and so does every later call in that
process. The library deletes the key when it unloads with no pool left,
so a cycle of dlopen(3) and dlclose(3) gives the key back.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
num_threads    Number of worker threads (must be >= 1)
queue_capacity 0 for unbounded; N > 0 for bounded. ccol_invalid_size
               is refused.
mprocs         Custom allocator, or NULL for malloc/free
err_str        Optional. It gets a static error string on failure
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new ctpool handle on success.
Returns CTPOOL_INVALID on failure, and sets *err_str if err_str is
not NULL. The call fails, and allocates nothing, when num_threads is
0 or when queue_capacity is ccol_invalid_size. ccol_invalid_size is
the error value of a size, not a capacity; pass 0 for an unbounded
queue.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The handle that this function gives is an opaque VALUE, not a pointer.
Do not cast it to void* or from void*, and do not use it as an address.
Compare it with CTPOOL_INVALID; because CTPOOL_INVALID is 0, a test such
as if (!pool) is also correct.
A future always uses the standard malloc and free, whatever mprocs is.
Each submission has a small internal task node behind it, which the
library recycles through a cache in each pool instead of allocating and
freeing it through mprocs on every call. A caller, such as a tracking
or measuring allocator, therefore cannot depend on mprocs to see one
allocation and one free for each submission: it sees a call only when
the cache is empty or already full, and when the pool trims the cache.
The cache keeps the nodes that the recent traffic of the pool needed,
so a burst of submissions of the same depth as an earlier one
allocates no node. It holds at most max(num_threads * 4, 32768) idle
nodes. At every 256th point where the pool goes idle, it gives back to
mprocs half of what it holds above num_threads * 4, unless the traffic
since the previous such point emptied it, so a cache that nothing needs
halves at each of those points, down to num_threads * 4.
ctpool_destroy does a drain shutdown itself when the caller called
neither ctpool_shutdown_drain nor ctpool_shutdown_immediate before it.
Before it frees anything, ctpool_destroy waits for every in-flight
resolved use of the handle (a ctpool_submit, ctpool_wait or
ctpool_pending_count call that runs at the same time) to stop, so the
caller does not need to prove on its own that no other thread still
uses the pool.

There are two supported ways to combine fork(2) with cthreadpool: a
fork(2) before the creation of a pool, or the creation of a pool
followed by a fork(2) with an exec(3) right after it. A child that
keeps running with no exec(3) in between, while a pool created before
the fork is still live, is not a supported pattern.
On top of that, the library adds a standing defence: a fresh call to
ccol_create_cthread_pool_mp, ctpool_submit, ctpool_try_submit,
ctpool_timed_submit or ctpool_destroy, or to one of the future, wait
and shutdown variants, never hangs on a lock. This holds for a call
from any thread, in the parent or in a child that forked a moment ago,
even when the lock belongs to another thread that held it at the
instant of the fork and that may no longer exist, because fork(2)
duplicates only the thread that calls it.
A pool that is already live across the fork is a different matter,
whatever that defence does. The child does not get copies of its
worker threads, so an inherited pool can run no queued task and no
future task there. Treat such a pool as inert in the child, and do not
submit to it. ctpool_wait on such a pool in the child returns at once
instead of waiting for progress that can never happen.
ctpool_destroy still discards any work that was in the queue at the
moment of the fork, and cancels every queued future, so a caller that
is blocked in ctpool_future_get on one of them wakes up instead of
hanging. ctpool_destroy does this before it frees the memory of the
pool, even with no explicit shutdown call before it.
.fi
